[STEP 1] Recruitment
Group 1 -  Initial Assessment Team (IAT)
 Member 1 (Infectious Disease Specialist (Lead)): Specializes in the diagnosis and treatment of viral infections, analyzes the initial viral infection case, and assesses the patient's symptoms and history to identify potential causes and necessary interventions.
 Member 2 (Geriatric Medicine Specialist): Provides insight into the unique health considerations and comorbidities affecting elderly patients, evaluates the patient's overall health, and assesses any potential complications from the viral infection.
 Member 3 (Pulmonologist): Focuses on the patient's respiratory symptoms and the implications of the dry cough; evaluates pulmonary function and suggests initial investigations or treatments required for respiratory distress.

Group 2 -  Diagnostic Evidence Team (DET)
 Member 1 (Virologist (Lead)): Examines the specifics of the viral infection, correlates the PCR test results with known viral characteristics (e.g., helical capsid, segmented genome), and provides insights into the potential epidemiology of the virus.
 Member 2 (Clinical Microbiologist): Collaborates to interpret laboratory results and identifies any secondary infections or complications arising from the primary viral infection, providing data that could influence management decisions.
 Member 3 (Radiologist): Assesses imaging studies (if performed) to evaluate potential lung involvement or complications from the virus, aiding in diagnosing pneumonia or other respiratory conditions that may complicate the case.

Group 3 -  Final Review and Decision Team (FRDT)
 Member 1 (Senior Consultant in Infectious Diseases (Lead)): Guides the overall management plan based on findings from both the IAT and DET, taking into account the characteristics of the virus and the patient’s health status.
 Member 2 (Epidemiologist): Contributes knowledge regarding the transmission dynamics of the identified virus, interpreting why some family members may have symptomatic infections while others do not, including possible factors like immunity or exposure.
 Member 3 (Clinical Decision Specialist): Integrates information from the various teams to formulate a comprehensive treatment plan, ensuring all aspects of the patient care are coordinated across specialties while monitoring for any changes in patient status.


